Marconi Server shares this computer's screen with the Marconi viewer on another computer.

It has to run inside your signed-in desktop, because that is where the screen is. It shares an X11 desktop; a
Wayland-only desktop has no screen the server can read.

Start it from the applications menu ("Marconi Server"), or by typing marconi-server. A small icon appears in the
system tray, and the first time it asks you to choose an access password. Nobody can connect until one is set.

To start it every time you sign in, run once, as yourself (not with sudo):

    systemctl --user enable --now marconi-server.service

To stop starting it at sign-in:

    systemctl --user disable --now marconi-server.service

Then, on the other computer, connect to marconi:// followed by this computer's name or address. The server listens
on port 1895 unless you change it from the tray icon.

marconi-server-console is the same server without the tray icon, for trying it from a terminal
(marconi-server-console --help lists its options).

Settings, the server's certificate and its log files are in ~/.local/share/marconi-server (the logs in its "logs"
folder: one file per run, the newest 20 are kept). Nothing secret is written to the logs.
